Sky News has commissioned a labour  members poll from YouGov as we enter the  final phase of the race and for the  first time we've been able to include  the views of trade unionists and others  eligible to vote in this poll and the  results couldn't be clearer the poll  suggests sakir stahma wins convincingly  in the first round by securing over 50  percent of the vote Rebecca long Bailey  is trailing in second place on 31% and  Lisa Nandi quick far behind on 16% the  figures suggest sakir is doing a smidgen  better that he was in mid-january when  this poll was last done  look how convincingly the shadow brexit  secretary wins by in so many of the key  categories now what you're looking at  now is the size of the lead securing  choice over miss long bailey amongst  different groups he does better amongst  women than men although he wins both  convincingly older members are more in  favor of sakir as our remain voters and  intriguingly trade unionists remember  just how strongly the trade unions used  to support Jeremy Corbyn while he was in  office but are now voting for change but  look here Rebecca long bailey has a lead  amongst labour members who voted for  brexit in 2016 and a huge advantage  amongst labour members who are part of  momentum there's one other revealing bit  of data here this shows how labour  members are likely to vote in this  contest depends on when they joined the  party now if you're in labour in the  Tony Blair Gordon Brown or Ed Miliband  areas you want Kearse dama  however if you joined while jeremy  corbyn was in the hot seat  you're more likely to favor Rebecca long  bailey but look at this if you join  since the December election drubbing and  the campaign's think around a hundred  thousand people have signed up since  then then you are overwhelmingly in  favor of Kearse Tama rather than the  korbinite choice of Rebecca long bailey  it appears people who want labour to  change direction have joined up in  droves in the last two months to take  back control from the corbin wing of the  party
 

